# C# Documentation Standards for Spellwright

Defines mandatory documentation standards for all C# scripts in the Spellwright project, following official C# XML documentation guidelines and Unity/DOTS best practices.

## Core Documentation Principles

### 1. XML Documentation Format

All public and internal types, members, and methods **MUST** use C# XML documentation comments with triple-slash `///` format.

**Format:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Brief description of the type or member.
/// </summary>
public class ExampleClass { }
```

**Never use:**

- Single-line comments `//` for API documentation
- Multi-line comments `/* */` for API documentation
- Inline comments for public member descriptions

### 2. Mandatory Documentation Targets

**MUST be documented:**

- All public classes, structs, and interfaces
- All public and internal methods
- All public properties and fields
- All ECS components (IComponentData)
- All ECS systems (ISystem, SystemBase)
- All Jobs (IJob, IJobEntity)
- All enum types and their values
- All delegates and events

**Optional documentation:**

- Private methods (recommended for complex logic)
- Private fields (only if non-obvious purpose)

### 3. Documentation Completeness

**Minimum required tags:**

- `<summary>` - Brief description (1-3 sentences)
- `<param>` - For each method parameter
- `<returns>` - For methods with non-void return
- `<exception>` - For methods that can throw exceptions
- `<remarks>` - For important implementation details (when needed)

## Standard XML Tags Reference

### Essential Tags

| Tag                  | Purpose                           | Required For       | Example                                                                               |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `<summary>`          | Brief description (1-3 sentences) | All public APIs    | `/// <summary>Defines movement data for projectile spells.</summary>`                 |
| `<param>`            | Parameter description             | All parameters     | `/// <param name="entity">The entity to move.</param>`                                |
| `<returns>`          | Return value description          | Non-void methods   | `/// <returns>The final damage value after modifiers.</returns>`                      |
| `<exception>`        | Exception documentation           | Methods that throw | `/// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException">Thrown when entity is null.</exception>` |
| `<remarks>`          | Implementation details            | When needed        | `/// <remarks>Runs in SimulationSystemGroup after physics.</remarks>`                 |
| `<value>`            | Property value description        | Properties         | `/// <value>Speed in units/second. Range: 0.1f to 100f.</value>`                      |
| `<example>`          | Usage example                     | Complex APIs       | `/// <example><code>var spell = CreateProjectile();</code></example>`                 |
| `<c>`                | Inline code term                  | In text            | `Sets the <c>Lifetime</c> component`                                                  |
| `<see cref=""/>`     | Code reference                    | Cross-references   | `See also <see cref="HealSystem"/>`                                                   |
| `<seealso cref=""/>` | Related reference                 | Related links      | `/// <seealso cref="HomingModifierSystem"/>`                                          |

### Tag Rules

**`<summary>` Rules:**

- Start with active verbs: "Represents", "Defines", "Calculates", "Processes"
- Use present tense
- 1-3 sentences max
- Don't repeat the member name
- **Bad:** `/// <summary>ProjectileComponent</summary>`
- **Good:** `/// <summary>Defines movement data for projectile-type spell generators.</summary>`

**`<param>` Rules:**

- One tag per parameter, match name exactly
- Start with "The" or "A/An"
- Describe purpose and valid values
- Example: `/// <param name="offset">The movement offset in world space.</param>`

**`<returns>` Rules:**

- Describe what is returned, not just the type
- Include range or possible values when relevant
- Mention null conditions for reference types

**Cross-References:**

- Use fully qualified names when ambiguous: `<see cref="UnityEngine.Transform"/>`
- For generics, use curly braces: `<see cref="DynamicBuffer{T}"/>`

## Unity-Specific Documentation Patterns

### MonoBehaviour/ScriptableObject

**Required documentation:**

- Component purpose and where to attach
- Required dependencies
- Inspector-configurable fields with valid ranges
- Unity messages used (Awake, Start, Update, etc.)

**Example:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Handles player input for spell casting. Attach to player GameObject.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Requires: PlayerController. Unity Messages: Update, OnEnable, OnDisable
/// </remarks>
public class SpellCastingInput : MonoBehaviour
{
    /// <summary>Key for casting primary spell. Configure in Inspector (default: Mouse0).</summary>
    [SerializeField] private KeyCode castKey = KeyCode.Mouse0;
}
```

### ECS Components (IComponentData)

**Required documentation:**

- What the component represents
- Which systems read/write it
- Performance characteristics (blittable, burst-compatible)
- Data constraints and valid ranges

**Example:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Remaining lifetime of a spell entity. Entity destroyed when reaches zero.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Unmanaged, Burst-compatible. Updated by: LifetimeSystem. Blittable, cache-friendly.
/// </remarks>
public struct Lifetime : IComponentData
{
    /// <summary>Time in seconds before destruction. Negative = unlimited.</summary>
    /// <value>Must be non-negative or -1 for unlimited.</value>
    public float Value;
}
```

### ECS Systems (ISystem)

**Required documentation:**

- System purpose (verb: "Processes", "Updates", "Handles")
- Components queried
- System group and update order
- Burst compilation status
- Threading model
- Performance characteristics

**Example:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Processes projectile movement for spell entities with ProjectileComponent.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Group: SimulationSystemGroup, After: TransformSystemGroup
/// Burst: Yes, Threading: Parallel (IJobEntity), Performance: O(n)
/// </remarks>
[BurstCompile]
[UpdateInGroup(typeof(SimulationSystemGroup))]
[UpdateAfter(typeof(TransformSystemGroup))]
public partial struct ProjectileMovementSystem : ISystem
{
    /// <summary>Initializes system state and queries.</summary>
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnCreate(ref SystemState state) { }

    /// <summary>Updates projectile positions every frame.</summary>
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state) { }
}
```

### ECS Jobs (IJobEntity)

**Required documentation:**

- Job purpose and execution model
- Burst compilation status
- Component access (Read/Write/Read-only)
- Thread safety

**Example:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Parallel job updating projectile positions based on velocity.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Burst: Yes, Schedule: Parallel, Access: RW LocalTransform, RO Velocity
/// </remarks>
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct MoveProjectilesJob : IJobEntity
{
    public float DeltaTime;

    public void Execute(ref LocalTransform transform, in Velocity velocity)
    {
        transform.Position += velocity.Value * DeltaTime;
    }
}
```

## Spellwright Architecture Documentation

### Generator/Modifier/Payload Pattern

Document how components fit into the spell architecture:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Projectile generator for the Spellwright spell system.
/// Part of Generator layer - defines how spells originate and move.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Layer: Generator (Mandatory). Compatible: Homing/Pierce/Fork modifiers, all payloads.
/// Performance: Blittable, Burst-compatible. Valid Speed: 0.1f to 100f (recommended: 5-30f).
/// </remarks>
public struct ProjectileComponent : IComponentData
{
    /// <summary>Movement speed in units per second.</summary>
    public float Speed;
}
```

### JSON Baking Systems

Document relationship to JSON schema:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Bakes projectile generator JSON into ECS components.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Type: Baking System. Execution: One-time during spell creation.
/// JSON Schema: "generator.projectile". Output: ProjectileComponent, Velocity, LocalTransform.
/// Validation: Client-side before baking.
/// </remarks>
public partial class ProjectileBakingSystem : SystemBase
{
    /// <summary>Bakes projectile spell from validated JSON data.</summary>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException">Thrown when jsonData is null.</exception>
    /// <exception cref="FormatException">Thrown when JSON doesn't match schema.</exception>
    protected override void OnUpdate() { }
}
```

## Advanced Documentation Patterns

### Performance Documentation

Document performance characteristics for DOTS code:

**Include:**

- Time complexity (Big O notation)
- Memory allocation (zero GC)
- Burst compilation status
- Threading model
- Target performance metrics

**Example:**

```csharp
/// <summary>Processes spell collisions using Unity Physics.</summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Performance: O(n*m) where n=spells, m=avg hits. Zero GC (NativeArrays).
/// Burst: Yes. Threading: Parallel (IJobEntity). Cache-friendly sequential access.
/// Target: &lt;0.5ms for 1000 spells @60fps. Profiler: "Spell.Physics"
/// </remarks>
```

### Validation and Constraints

Document data validation:

```csharp
/// <summary>Validates spell JSON against Spellwright schema.</summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Three-layer validation: 1) API Function Calling (LLM), 2) Few-Shot Prompting (LLM), 3) Client-side (this).
/// </remarks>
public class SpellValidator
{
    /// <summary>Validates damage value is within range.</summary>
    /// <returns>True if valid; otherwise, false.</returns>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Valid: 1.0f to 1000.0f. Recommended: 10f to 100f. Must be positive and finite.
    /// </remarks>
    public bool ValidateDamage(float damage) { }
}
```

### Thread Safety

Document thread safety for jobs/parallel processing:

```csharp
/// <summary>Processes multiple spells in parallel using job system.</summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Thread Safety: RO Velocity/ProjectileComponent, RW LocalTransform (no shared writes).
/// Parallel Safe: Yes (no race conditions). ECB: Deferred structural changes.
/// Synchronization: Implicit via IJobEntity scheduling.
/// </remarks>
```

## Documentation Quality Checklist

- [ ] All public types have `<summary>` tags
- [ ] All method parameters have `<param>` tags
- [ ] All non-void methods have `<returns>` tags
- [ ] All exceptions documented with `<exception>` tags
- [ ] Cross-references use `<see cref=""/>` with correct syntax
- [ ] ECS components document which systems use them
- [ ] ECS systems document update order and burst compilation
- [ ] Performance-critical code includes performance remarks
- [ ] Unity-specific attributes are explained
- [ ] No spelling or grammar errors

## Tools and Validation

**IDE Integration:**

- Visual Studio: Enable XML documentation warnings (CS1591)
- Rider: Enable "Missing XML comment" inspections
- VS Code: Install C# XML Documentation extension

**Build-Time:** Treat documentation warnings as errors in CI/CD pipeline

**AI-Assisted:** Always review for technical accuracy, verify code references, validate performance claims, check Unity/DOTS terminology

## Best Practices

**Do:**

- Write descriptive titles with scope: `/// <summary>Projectile movement speed in units/second. Valid: 0.1f-100f.</summary>`
- Document all public types, methods, parameters, and return values
- Include performance characteristics for DOTS code
- Document thread safety for parallel systems
- Keep documentation synchronized with code changes
- Explain **why**, not just **what**

**Don't:**

- Use redundant descriptions: `/// <summary>Gets or sets the speed.</summary>` (adds nothing)
- Write vague descriptions: `/// <summary>Does stuff with entity.</summary>`
- Forget XML escaping: Use `<see cref="List{T}"/>` not `List<T>`
- Copy-paste without reviewing for accuracy
- Leave documentation outdated after refactoring

## Important Notes

- Documentation is **mandatory**, not optional
- Write for developers who are unfamiliar with the code
- Keep documentation synchronized with code changes
- Document **why**, not just **what** - explain design decisions
- Use proper XML syntax - validate with IDE tools
- Performance documentation is critical for DOTS code
- All documentation must be in **English**
- Document public API surface area completely
- Private implementation details are optional but encouraged
- Update documentation when refactoring code
